/-! # The mediant step of the general-slope gluing identity

`HJO/Shuffle/MellitLhsSlopeBase.lean` and `HJO/Shuffle/MellitLhsSlopeThreeFour.lean`
settle the `α = (1)` base case of `HJO.Mellit.lhsRewrite_sweepWitness` at the two smallest slopes
with `a > 1`. This file carries out the **mediant induction** those two instances exhibit: it states
the general clause at a general `f`, proves the `a = 1` column, and reduces the mediant step to a
single identity about the `N` term of `z_1`.

## Main definitions

* `HJO.Mellit.LhsSlope`: the general clause at `(a, b)` — for every `f ∈ Λ`,
  `C(Q_{a,b}f) = (-1)^{b+1}d_-(Ξ^{(1)}_{a,b}(y_1d^*_+{}^{(0)}(Cf)))`. The `f` is general because
  `HJO.Sym.Qop`'s recursion evaluates `Q_{r,s}` at `Q_{a-r,b-s}(1) ≠ 1`, so an `f = 1` statement
  cannot induct.
* `HJO.Mellit.NTermGlue`: the residual identity of the mediant step, isolated.

## Main results

* `HJO.Mellit.lhsSlope_one_left`: the clause at `(1, n)` at a general `f` — the `a = 1` column and
  the base of the induction, from `HJO.Sweep.dop_eq_neg_dminusCM_auxVar_pow_dplusStar`.
* `HJO.Mellit.dminus_slopeOperator_mediant`: **the mediant step computed**, with no hypothesis on
  the parameters: given the clause at both Stern–Brocot parents, the sweep side at the mediant is
  `-(qu)^{-1}(q/(1-q))` times `(-1)^{p₂+r₂}C(Q_rQ_pf)` minus the `N` term.
* `HJO.Mellit.lhsSlope_mediant`: the clause at the mediant, from the clause at both parents and
  `NTermGlue`. **All of the scalar and sign bookkeeping of the induction is discharged here**; the
  scalar identity spent is `((1-q)(1-u))^{-1} = (qu)^{-1}(q/(1-q))(u/(1-u))`, and it is the only
  place `q ∉ {0,1}`, `u ∉ {0,1}` are used.
* `HJO.Mellit.nTermGlue_of_lhsSlope`: the converse — the clause at the two parents and at the
  mediant forces `NTermGlue`. So `NTermGlue` is not one convenient sufficient condition among many
  but the **exact** residual content of the step.
* `HJO.Mellit.lhsBase_of_lhsSlope`: the clause implies the base case in the shape
  `HJO.Mellit.qop_apply_one_of_lhsComputes` asks for, so `HJO.Mellit.lhsBase_two_three` and
  `HJO.Mellit.lhsBase_three_four` are its `(2,3)` and `(3,4)` instances read at `f = 1`.
* `HJO.Mellit.auxVar_mul_dminus_two_dplusStar_one`, `HJO.Mellit.nTerm_eq_of_lhsSlope`,
  `HJO.Mellit.nTermGlue_iff_lhsSlope`: **the `N` term eliminated.** `y_1N = P - ((1-q)/q)y_1z_1` and
  `y_1z_1` is the letter pair, so `NTermGlue` is equivalent to the clause at the mediant and is not
  a statement about `z_1`'s second half.
* `HJO.Mellit.lhsSlope_of_prod_elemSymm`: the clause need only be checked on products of `e_n`,
  both sides being `L`-linear in `f`. So a general `f` is bookkeeping and the slope is the content.
* `HJO.Sym.dop_elemSymm_mul`: the **full Pieri rule** `D_k(e_nf) = ∑_{s≤n}κ(e_s)e_{n-s}D_{k+s}f`,
  generalising `HJO.Sym.dop_elemSymm_one_mul` from `e_1` to every `e_n`, with no hypothesis on
  `q, u`. This is the `Λ`-side lemma a general-`f` computation needs.

## Where the induction stands

The step is **not** blocked on bookkeeping. `HJO.Mellit.lhsSlope_mediant` is a theorem, so the two
signs, the mediant factorisation and the mismatch between `HJO.Sym.Qop`'s `M^{-1}` and
`HJO.Sweep.slopeOperator`'s `(u(1-q))^{-1}` all reconcile exactly, the difference
`M^{-1} - (u(1-q))^{-1} = (2u-1)/((1-q)u(1-u))` being absorbed by the `N` term and by nothing else.

What is left is one operator identity, `HJO.Mellit.NTermGlue`:

`d_-Ξ_r(y_1·N(Ξ_p(y_1d^*_+Cf))) = (-1)^{p₂+r₂}((1-u)^{-1}C(Q_rQ_pf) - (u/(1-u))C(Q_pQ_rf))`.

Two facts about it record why it is hard.

**It does not follow from either mediant factorisation.** `HJO.Mellit.slopeEval_mediant` and
`HJO.Mellit.slopeEval_mediant_rev` give `Ξ_rYZΞ_p = Ξ_pZYΞ_r`, and `YZ` is
`-(u(1-q))^{-1}(P - y_1N)` with `P := y_1d^*_+{}^{(0)}d_-^{(1)}`: only the *difference* `P - y_1N`
lies in the monoid generated by the two letters, so neither `P` nor `y_1N` alone is constrained by
the word combinatorics. The `P` half is exactly what the induction hypothesis consumes
(`HJO.Mellit.dminus_slopeOperator_mediant`), and the `N` half is what is left over.

**It is not local in `Ξ_p(y_1d^*_+Cf)`.** `N` on a general element of `V_1` *is* computable —
`HJO.Sweep.zopOneStar_one_auxVar_pow_mul_C` says `N(y_1^mCA) = B_m(d^*_+{}^{(0)}(CA))`
coefficientwise in `y_1` — and so is the outer `d_-`. But the right-hand side contains `Q_pQ_rf`,
which is `Ξ_p` re-run on the *different* vector `y_1d^*_+C(Q_rf)`; no information about the single
element `Ξ_p(y_1d^*_+Cf)` can determine it. So the identity is a commutation statement between `Ξ_p`
and the outer `d_-`/`y_1d^*_+` structure — in effect the elliptic Hall relation for these operators
— and not a computation with the evaluations proved so far.

At `(2,3)`, where `r = Split 2 3 = (1,1)` and `Ξ_r = 1`, `Q_p = D_2` and `Q_r = D_1`, it reads
`d_-(y_1N(y_1^2d^*_+Cf)) = (1-u)^{-1}C(D_1D_2f) - (u/(1-u))C(D_2D_1f)`; at `f = 1` that is
`B_1(e_2) = (1-u)^{-1}D_1(e_2) + u(1-u)^{-1}D_2(e_1)`, which the closed forms
`HJO.Sym.dop_one_elemSymm_two` and the Pieri rule `HJO.Sym.dop_elemSymm_one_mul` satisfy identically
in `q` and `u` — that is `HJO.Mellit.nTermGlue_two_three_apply_one`, and it is the check that the
coefficients `(1-u)^{-1}` and `u/(1-u)` above are the right *pair*: `HJO.Mellit.lhsSlope_mediant`
constrains only their difference, so a wrong split between them passes every other check here.

**The `N` term is not independent data, so there is nothing smaller to prove.**
`HJO.Mellit.auxVar_mul_dminus_two_dplusStar_one` shows `y_1N = P - ((1-q)/q)y_1z_1` on the whole
total space, and `y_1z_1 = -qu·(YZ)` is the letter pair itself; so
`HJO.Mellit.nTerm_eq_of_lhsSlope` evaluates the `N` term as the `P` term plus `u(1-q)` times the
sweep side at the mediant. Consequently `HJO.Mellit.nTermGlue_iff_lhsSlope`: the residual identity
is *equivalent* to the clause at the mediant. **`NTermGlue` is therefore not a statement about the
second half of `z_1` at all**, and no evaluation of `N` — however general — can discharge it. The
content is the commutator recursion of `HJO.Sym.Qop` for the sweep-side composites
`f ↦ ct(d_-(Ξ_{a,b}(y_1d^*_+Cf)))`, and nothing else. At `(2,3)` it reads
`d_-(y_1z_1(y_1^2d^*_+Cf)) = qu·C(Q_{2,3}f)`, which is the clause at `(2,3)` again.

## Which of the two generalisations is the hard one: the slope, not `f`

`HJO.Mellit.lhsSlope_of_prod_elemSymm` settles this. The clause is `L`-linear in `f`
(`HJO.Mellit.lhsSlopeCarrier`) and the products of the `e_{i+1}` span `Λ`
(`HJO.Sym.adjoin_elemSymm_eq_top` with `Algebra.adjoin_eq_span`), so **a general `f` is
bookkeeping**: it is enough to prove the clause on `e`-monomials. Both halves are then known in
closed form on such an `f`:

* sweep side — `HJO.Sweep.dplusStar_C_prod_elemSymm` expands `d^*_+` on an arbitrary `e`-monomial
  unconditionally, and `d^*_+` is an `AlgHom` by `rfl`
  (`HJO.Sweep.dplusStarAlg_eq_dplusStar`), so a *list* product is the matching shape, via
  `map_list_prod`;
* `Q` side — `HJO.Sym.dop_elemSymm_mul`, the full Pieri rule
  `D_k(e_nf) = ∑_{s≤n}κ(e_s)e_{n-s}D_{k+s}f` proved below. Without it, the basic operator against a
  product is known only for `e_1` (`HJO.Sym.dop_elemSymm_one_mul`), so a general `e`-monomial could
  not be pushed through `HJO.Sym.Qop`'s commutator at all. It is checked at `n = 1` and at `n = 2`
  against the two known values, by `HJO.Sym.dop_elemSymm_one_mul_of_pieri` and
  `HJO.Sym.dop_one_elemSymm_two_of_pieri`.

The **slope** is the content: nothing here reduces a slope to smaller ones except
`HJO.Mellit.lhsSlope_mediant`, whose residual hypothesis is the clause itself. So proving the clause
at general `f` for one fixed slope — the `(2,3)` case, whose two sides are both available — is a
finite computation that will *not* generalise in the slope, and the uniform statement needs the
commutator relation above.

## The column this file does *not* reach

`HJO.Mellit.lhsSlope_mediant` needs `1 ≤ p₂` and `1 ≤ r₂`, because
`HJO.Mellit.slopeWord_mediant` is stated for two positive coprime pairs. By
`HJO.Mellit.isSBParent_pos` that holds exactly when `a, b ≥ 2`. So the three cases of the induction
are the three of the induction behind `HJO.Mellit.euclid`, and their status here is:

* `(1,n)`: **proved**, at a general `f`, by `HJO.Mellit.lhsSlope_one_left`;
* `(a,b)` with `a, b ≥ 2`: **reduced** to `NTermGlue`, by `HJO.Mellit.lhsSlope_mediant`;
* `(a,1)` with `a ≥ 2`: **not treated at a general `f`**, but settled at the two smallest
  coefficients, uniformly in `a`: at `f = 1` by
  `HJO.Sweep.dminus_straightMonomial_slopeArg_one` with `HJO.Sym.qop_apply_one_of_snd_one`
  (`MellitStraightMonomial.lean`), and at `f = e_1` by
  `HJO.Sweep.lhsSlopeAt_succ_one_elemSymm_one` (`MellitVertexStepGeneral.lean`), where the value
  does depend on `a` and so is a real test of the slope. What follows is why a general `f` needs
  its own residual identity. Its Stern–Brocot parents are `(a-1,1)` and `(1,0)`, and
  `Ξ_{1,0}` is outside the range of `HJO.Mellit.slopeEval_one_left`, so the mediant step cannot be
  applied; the recursion available there is `HJO.Mellit.slopeEval_succ_right`,
  `Ξ_{a,1} = Z·Ξ_{a-1,1}`, whose `Q`-side is `Q_{a,1} = M^{-1}(Q_{a-1,1}D_0 - D_0Q_{a-1,1})`
  (`HJO.Sym.split_snd_one`), and it needs its own residual identity, with one `Z` rather than a
  `YZ` pair. This column is not optional: at `(3,2)` the parents are `(1,1)` and `(2,1)`, so every
  slope with `a > b` reaches it.

## Genericity

`q ∉ {0,1}` and `u ∉ {0,1}`, exactly as at `(2,3)` and `(3,4)`: `(qu)^{-1}` from
`HJO.Sweep.slopeOperator`, `(1-q)^{-1}` from `HJO.Sweep.zop`, `((1-q)(1-u))^{-1}` from
`HJO.Sym.Qop`. `u ≠ 1` is necessary on a curve, not at isolated points
(`HJO.Mellit.not_lhsBase_two_three_of_u_eq_one`). `HJO.Mellit.dminus_slopeOperator_mediant` and
`HJO.Mellit.lhsSlope_one_left` need no hypothesis on the parameters at all.

## Implementation notes

The identity is proved at `a = 1` by `HJO.Sweep.dop_eq_neg_dminusCM_auxVar_pow_dplusStar`; its
general-slope form is what `HJO.Mellit.LhsSlope` states, and it is not proved here, only reduced.
`HJO.Mellit.lhsSlope_mediant` carries hypotheses the general-slope statement does not have —
`NTermGlue`, and the value of `HJO.Sym.Split` at the slope.

## References

Declarations involved: `HJO.Sweep.dop_eq_neg_dminusCM_auxVar_pow_dplusStar` (general slope),
`HJO.Mellit.lhsRewrite_sweepWitness` (base case), `HJO.Sym.Qop`, `HJO.Sweep.slopeOperator`,
`HJO.Sweep.zop`, `HJO.Mellit.slopeWord_mediant`. Transcribing A. Mellit, *Toric braids and
`(m, n)`-parking functions*, §3, §6.
-/

